Kenner Gordon wrote:


> You can make a piece of armor rustproof by reading a scroll of enchant
> armor while confused and wearing the armor in question, unless I'm
> mistaken. (Only one piece of armor while be rustproofed, selected
> randomly out of those you're wearing, so if you want to rustproof a
> particular piece of armor, take off all other armor before reading the
> scroll.)

This is, in my opinion, one thing which is blatantly wrong with the 
game design in nethack.

If being confused means that ordinary actions like walking don't 
produce consistent results, then IMO it should also mean that actions
like reading a scroll don't produce consistent results.  Reading a 
scroll while confused ought to produce one of at least eight 
different equiprobable results, more than half of them bad.  Or 
maybe it should just fail.

To the point, confusion is a status effect that there is good
reason to avoid or try to rapidly cure.  There is no game-consistent 
reason for an unspoiled player to expect it to ever be a good thing.  

I don't mind decisions with tactical tradeoffs, but this kind of 
feature (negative status effect such as confusion or curse being the 
method to achieve a useful result) is beneficial *only* to the 
spoiled player, and as such ought to be excised or modified. 

